messages:
Ben: Hey — seeing a weird pattern from Node SDK streaming clients. When the server is under load and we send partial JSON chunks, clients sometimes reconnect and immediately get 401 during the reconnect handshake. If we retry ~200ms later with the same refresh token flow it succeeds. Repro snippet (super minimal):
```js
const res = await fetch(url, { headers: { Authorization: `Bearer ${token}` } });
const reader = res.body.getReader();
const dec = new TextDecoder();
let buf = '';
while (true) {
  const { value, done } = await reader.read();
  if (done) break;
  buf += dec.decode(value, { stream: true });
  // we split on newline delim but chunks can cut JSON mid-object
  let idx;
  while ((idx = buf.indexOf('\n')) >= 0) {
    const line = buf.slice(0, idx).trim();
    buf = buf.slice(idx + 1);
    if (!line) continue;
    try {
      const obj = JSON.parse(line);
      // handle obj
    } catch (e) {
      // partial — keep buffering
    }
  }
}
```
Logs: `reconnect start -> auth refresh -> fetch -> 401` then `retry after 200ms -> fetch -> 200`. Anyone seen auth races on reconnects when streams are choppy/backpressured?

Maya: have seen similar with long-lived connections where token rotation happens while the server still considers the socket tied to old token. A few quick checks: are you reusing an Agent that's pooling sockets? Node's http.Agent can keep sockets alive and server might apply auth per-socket on first request. Try disabling keepAlive or using a fresh agent on reconnect. Also, do you use signed URLs for SSE in browsers? those are single-use sometimes.

Sam: +1 on Agent. Also if the server uses per-connection auth cache, aborting without draining can leave server state confused. On reconnect do: `controller.abort(); await drainOrTimeout(res.body);` or explicitly set `Connection: close` for reconnect attempts. That forces a fresh TCP handshake with new headers.

Hiro: Another angle — if partial JSON triggers your parser to stall, you might be hitting backpressure where your client can't accept more data and the server pauses the auth rotation/refresh flow. Two practical mitigations we've used: 1) heartbeat frames from server so client can tell server is alive (`:hb\n\n` for SSE or `\n` ping for ndjson), 2) on client-side, implement a small read-window: after N bytes buffered, pause consuming upstream (or apply backpressure to your queue) and avoid reconnect spamming.

Ben: Good context — we're not using keepAlive intentionally but we are reading with a tiny buffer loop (above). The server does send comment heartbeats sometimes but they can arrive inside a chunk and get glued to JSON if boundary handling is naive. Does anyone have a robust partial-JSON reassembly pattern you trust?

Maya: One pattern: always accumulate into a buffer and only split on a delimiter you control (prefer explicit record separators, e.g., use `\u001e` RS char or `\n\n`). If you can change server, switch to SSE comment heartbeats (`: keepalive\n\n`) and on client, treat any line starting with `:` as heartbeat. Also prefer streaming parsers (e.g., `clarinet`/`sax` style) if payloads can be huge or arbitrarily chunked.

Sam: For auth retry strategy — on reconnect 401: immediately refresh token, then exponential backoff retry starting at 100ms, cap ~2s, and on each retry force a new Agent/Connection. Optionally jitter retries to avoid thundering herd. That combo has been reliable for us.

Hiro: Quick code tweak we used to force fresh sockets on Node fetch (node 18+):
```js
import { Agent } from 'http';
const agent = new Agent({ keepAlive: false });
await fetch(url, { agent, headers: { Authorization: `Bearer ${token}` } });
```
Also, when you refresh tokens concurrently, serialize refresh requests (only one inflight refresh) to avoid race conditions where two reconnects race to set a token and one loses.

Ben: Awesome — I'll try: 1) disable keepAlive for reconnects, 2) add token-refresh serialization, 3) add small jittered backoff on 401 + force new agent. If that still flakes, I'll flip server to send explicit RS `\u001e` between records.

Maya: If you want, I can put together a tiny harness (local server that purposely cuts JSON mid-object and injects heartbeat/comment lines) so we can reproduce without prod. Worth an integration test. :+1:

dev-bot: created issue #2456: "node streaming reconnect 401 on partial-json/backpressure" and assigned to Ben.

Ben: Thanks — will follow up on the issue with results. Appreciate the quick checks. :rocket:
